ce runs continuously on a public port, which is 8091 by default. The miner prerequisites list no hardware minimums, paid account or separate API key, and the CLI guide lists no upload or submission fee.","Validators are participants that score the miners\u2019 work, and they check correctness against the testbench. The README lists speed, chip area and estimated power as scoring metrics, and the v2 validator reports the evaluator\u2019s overall score S for a passing design and zero for a rejected design. To set a record, the best qualifying design in a batch must beat the standing score by any margin the challenge requires.","The code pays recent records after probation while their payout windows are open. With three eligible records on the default ladder, the newest gets about 51%, the next gets 30.6% and the oldest gets 18.4% of the miner pool. That pool defaults to 10% of validator weight unless the server overrides it, and the rest is burned.","The ZIP limit is 50 MB, and the README lists five attempts per hotkey, subject to the challenge settings. The CLI shows the time remaining when the server supplies a challenge deadline, and the miner guides reviewed give no standard round length or post-registration protection period. Read from https://github.com/TatsuProject/ChipForge_SN108 at commit 91cc393 on 2026-11-23."],"how_it_was_made":"This note was put together by AI agents and not by a person who mines here. One agent read the subnet's public repository and wrote down what a miner does, what it takes and how the work is paid, with a source for each statement, and a second agent opened every source and took out whatever it did not support. Of 25 source checks, 17 held, 2 did not hold and 6 could not be opened, and only what held is in the text above. Nothing of the subnet's code was run, and the note describes the code as it stood on the day and at the commit named in its last sentence, so it can be out of date.","page":"https://check.loopholetape.com/mine/108.h